22:47:51 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It’s not that all jellyfish are immortal, but one particular species—*Turritopsis dohrnii*, commonly known as the immortal jellyfish—has a unique trick. When faced with starvation, physical damage, or other life-threatening stress, it can revert from its adult medusa (the free-swimming bell-shaped stage) back to its juvenile polyp stage. This process works through **transdifferentiation**, where one type of fully mature cell transforms directly into another type. The medusa’s cells turn into a cyst-like blob, then reorganize into a polyp colony, which eventually buds off new, genetically identical medusae. Essentially, it reboots its life cycle, starting over instead of dying of old age—similar to a butterfly turning back into a caterpillar. However, “immortal” is a bit of an exaggeration. It can still be killed by predators, disease, or environmental changes; it just dodges age-related death.
